WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.592
Imagine waking up and finding that pulling on a jumper, turning over

00:00:03.692 --> 00:00:07.121
in bed, or getting out of a chair has become painfully difficult.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:10.228
The aching sits around both shoulders and both hips, and

00:00:10.328 --> 00:00:13.021
it's worst after rest, first thing in the morning.

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:16.876
This pattern has a name: polymyalgia rheumatica, or PMR.

00:00:16.976 --> 00:00:20.545
Taken literally, the name means many muscles, aching.

00:00:20.633 --> 00:00:22.992
But as we'll see, the muscles themselves

00:00:23.092 --> 00:00:25.328
are mostly not where the problem lies.

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:28.198
One note before we begin. This is an educational

00:00:28.298 --> 00:00:31.701
explainer, not medical advice. Nothing here should be used

00:00:31.801 --> 00:00:33.935
to start, stop or change a treatment.

00:00:36.470 --> 00:00:39.472
Let's peel a shoulder apart. At the centre is the ball at

00:00:39.572 --> 00:00:42.139
the top of the arm bone, beneath an arch of bone.

00:00:42.236 --> 00:00:45.969
Around the joint is its lining, the synovium. Tendons run over

00:00:46.069 --> 00:00:49.617
the top, carrying force. And between tendon and bone sits a

00:00:49.717 --> 00:00:53.141
bursa, a tiny fluid-lined cushion that lets things glide.

00:00:53.236 --> 00:00:55.737
And wrapped over all of it is the muscle itself.

00:00:55.836 --> 00:00:58.510
Ultrasound and MRI studies show that in PMR,

00:00:58.610 --> 00:01:02.355
inflammation concentrates around the joint, in bursae, tendon

00:01:02.455 --> 00:01:05.570
sheaths and joint linings, not in destroyed muscle.

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:10.210
That matters. I can't raise my arms could mean it hurts, the

00:01:10.310 --> 00:01:14.250
joint is stiff, or the muscle is genuinely weak. In PMR it's

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:18.695
usually the first two: painful, stiff movement, not lost strength.

00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:22.715
PMR is uncommon before the age of fifty, and much more

00:01:22.815 --> 00:01:26.861
typical after sixty-five. Women are affected more often than men.

00:01:26.936 --> 00:01:30.144
Fatigue, low appetite, weight loss, a mild fever or low

00:01:30.244 --> 00:01:33.331
mood can come too, but they happen in other illnesses

00:01:33.431 --> 00:01:36.158
as well. It's the overall pattern that matters.

00:01:38.322 --> 00:01:40.219
So why does it happen? PMR is an

00:01:40.319 --> 00:01:44.650
immune-mediated inflammatory disease. That describes what is happening,

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:47.333
not why it starts in one particular person.

00:01:47.422 --> 00:01:50.375
Picture a row of dominoes. We understand much of

00:01:50.475 --> 00:01:54.447
the later chain: immune signals, inflammation, symptoms. But the

00:01:54.547 --> 00:01:57.246
first domino, the trigger, is still unknown.

00:01:57.322 --> 00:01:59.560
No single infection, food, gene or life

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:01.958
event has been established as the cause.

00:02:02.655 --> 00:02:06.203
Genetic studies point to immune-related regions, such as HLA.

00:02:06.303 --> 00:02:09.972
But these are susceptibility signals, not predictions. Think of

00:02:10.072 --> 00:02:13.082
a landscape, not a switch: small influences tilt the

00:02:13.182 --> 00:02:16.671
ground, making inflammation more likely without deciding it.

00:02:17.355 --> 00:02:20.822
And association is not blame. Nobody causes their PMR by

00:02:20.922 --> 00:02:25.218
skipping exercise, eating the wrong breakfast, or being too stressed.

00:02:27.533 --> 00:02:31.413
Inflammation is a conversation between cells. Immune cells talk

00:02:31.513 --> 00:02:35.646
using signalling proteins called cytokines, and one important voice

00:02:35.746 --> 00:02:37.857
in PMR is interleukin six, or IL-6.

00:02:37.933 --> 00:02:41.448
IL-6 binds a receptor on a responding cell, together with

00:02:41.548 --> 00:02:45.127
a partner protein called gp130. Inside, a relay called the

00:02:45.227 --> 00:02:48.490
JAK–STAT pathway changes which genes are switched on.

00:02:48.566 --> 00:02:51.560
One place this matters is the liver. Prompted by

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:54.912
IL-6, it releases C-reactive protein, or CRP, one of

00:02:55.012 --> 00:02:57.683
the blood tests used to track inflammation.

00:02:57.766 --> 00:03:02.568
Now watch what treatment does. A glucocorticoid steroid, such as prednisolone,

00:03:02.668 --> 00:03:06.024
acts broadly, turning down much of the network at once.

00:03:06.099 --> 00:03:09.182
An IL-6 receptor blocker is more targeted. It cuts

00:03:09.282 --> 00:03:12.555
one specific link. And because CRP depends heavily on

00:03:12.655 --> 00:03:15.738
that signal, blocking it pushes CRP down directly.

00:03:15.833 --> 00:03:18.857
A subtlety follows. On these drugs, a low CRP doesn't

00:03:18.957 --> 00:03:22.158
prove that the disease, or an infection, has gone quiet.

00:03:22.258 --> 00:03:25.518
Dimming a warning light doesn't prove the system is safe.

00:03:28.101 --> 00:03:30.757
PMR also has a daily rhythm. Why does getting

00:03:30.857 --> 00:03:33.085
up feel so much worse than staying up?

00:03:33.167 --> 00:03:36.206
In a small, controlled study of untreated patients, pain and

00:03:36.306 --> 00:03:39.397
stiffness were greatest between roughly four and eight in the

00:03:39.497 --> 00:03:41.960
morning, and lowest around four in the afternoon.

00:03:42.034 --> 00:03:46.624
The body's own cortisol, an anti-inflammatory hormone, also rises and falls

00:03:46.724 --> 00:03:50.501
each day. It's tempting to blame too little cortisol, but that

00:03:50.601 --> 00:03:54.315
study found cortisol was higher in PMR than in people without

00:03:54.415 --> 00:03:57.942
it. The story is timing and demand, not a simple shortage.

00:03:58.634 --> 00:04:02.559
Morning stiffness over forty-five minutes is one feature researchers use

00:04:02.659 --> 00:04:06.137
to classify PMR. But it's no magic threshold: forty-four minutes

00:04:06.237 --> 00:04:09.156
doesn't rule it out, and forty-six doesn't rule it in.

00:04:11.552 --> 00:04:13.567
There is no single test for PMR. A

00:04:13.667 --> 00:04:16.989
diagnosis is assembled, by looking through four lenses.

00:04:17.085 --> 00:04:21.377
Pattern: age, where it hurts, morning stiffness, the examination. Blood

00:04:21.477 --> 00:04:24.656
tests: markers like CRP and ESR, plus tests for other

00:04:24.756 --> 00:04:29.482
explanations. Imaging: ultrasound can show inflamed bursae and tendon sheaths.

00:04:29.582 --> 00:04:33.503
And review: revisiting the diagnosis if the course stops fitting.

00:04:33.585 --> 00:04:36.730
Other conditions can look like PMR: older-onset

00:04:36.830 --> 00:04:40.803
rheumatoid arthritis, shoulder problems, muscle disease, an

00:04:40.903 --> 00:04:43.841
underactive thyroid, infection, even cancer.

00:04:44.518 --> 00:04:47.273
And a dramatic response to steroids supports the

00:04:47.373 --> 00:04:50.723
diagnosis, but it isn't proof. Steroids quiet inflammation

00:04:50.823 --> 00:04:53.577
in many diseases, and can hide the alternatives.

00:04:55.957 --> 00:04:59.448
There is one related condition that changes the urgency

00:04:59.548 --> 00:05:03.235
completely: giant cell arteritis, or GCA. It overlaps with

00:05:03.335 --> 00:05:06.827
PMR, but it is inflammation of the arteries themselves.

00:05:06.957 --> 00:05:10.328
When the artery wall becomes inflamed and thickens, the

00:05:10.428 --> 00:05:13.799
open channel inside, the lumen, narrows. Less blood can

00:05:13.899 --> 00:05:17.839
reach the tissues downstream, including those that serve vision.

00:05:18.557 --> 00:05:21.817
So these symptoms must not wait. New loss of vision, a

00:05:21.917 --> 00:05:26.422
curtain over vision, or double vision needs emergency assessment now, even

00:05:26.522 --> 00:05:30.032
if it has passed. A new headache, scalp tenderness, or jaw

00:05:30.132 --> 00:05:33.455
pain when chewing needs urgent assessment the same day.

00:05:33.523 --> 00:05:37.176
And a normal blood test, an existing PMR diagnosis, or already

00:05:37.276 --> 00:05:40.807
taking steroids does not make these symptoms safe to ignore.

00:05:41.490 --> 00:05:44.276
Doctors may start treatment for strongly suspected

00:05:44.376 --> 00:05:46.700
GCA before tests confirm it, because delay

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:49.412
can risk irreversible harm, including to sight.

00:05:51.654 --> 00:05:54.785
Treatment has two phases: control the inflammation, then

00:05:54.885 --> 00:05:57.842
carefully earn each reduction. It usually starts with

00:05:57.942 --> 00:06:01.189
a glucocorticoid steroid, commonly prednisolone in the UK.

00:06:01.787 --> 00:06:05.129
Some simple arithmetic shows why the last steps can feel

00:06:05.229 --> 00:06:08.510
the biggest. One unit from twenty is five percent. From

00:06:08.610 --> 00:06:12.075
ten, it's ten percent. And from five, it's twenty percent.

00:06:12.287 --> 00:06:15.032
That's arithmetic only, not a taper schedule. But it hints

00:06:15.132 --> 00:06:17.387
at a deeper point: there are two clocks running.

00:06:18.087 --> 00:06:21.261
Clock A is the disease: active, suppressed, tested as the

00:06:21.361 --> 00:06:24.593
dose comes down, and hopefully, remission. Clock B is your

00:06:24.693 --> 00:06:28.154
own cortisol system. Long-term steroids tell the brain to turn

00:06:28.254 --> 00:06:31.371
down the adrenal glands, which may need time to recover.

00:06:31.454 --> 00:06:34.020
The two clocks are not synchronised. Disease can be

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:36.949
controlled while the adrenal system is still suppressed.

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:39.914
So feeling worse during a reduction could be

00:06:40.014 --> 00:06:43.506
relapse, steroid withdrawal, or adrenal insufficiency, and telling

00:06:43.606 --> 00:06:46.335
them apart needs assessment. It's also why prolonged

00:06:46.435 --> 00:06:49.001
steroid treatment must never be stopped abruptly.

00:06:51.202 --> 00:06:55.711
What about steroid-sparing medicines? Four trials asked four different questions,

00:06:55.811 --> 00:06:58.670
so we'll read each on its own, without ranking them.

00:06:59.268 --> 00:07:02.750
In SAPHYR, people with relapsing PMR got the IL-6 receptor

00:07:02.850 --> 00:07:06.641
blocker sarilumab plus a fourteen-week taper, or placebo plus a

00:07:06.741 --> 00:07:11.459
fifty-two-week taper. Sustained remission at one year: twenty-eight versus ten

00:07:11.559 --> 00:07:15.782
percent, an eighteen-point difference, with far less steroid used. But

00:07:15.882 --> 00:07:19.426
the endpoint included CRP, which this drug lowers directly.

00:07:20.002 --> 00:07:24.104
A newer trial gave methotrexate, twenty-five milligrams once a week,

00:07:24.204 --> 00:07:28.183
in recently diagnosed PMR. Eighty versus forty-six percent had low

00:07:28.283 --> 00:07:31.891
disease activity and no steroids at one year. Promising, but

00:07:31.991 --> 00:07:36.155
small. And note: for inflammatory disease, methotrexate is taken once

00:07:36.255 --> 00:07:39.739
weekly. Accidental daily dosing has caused fatal toxicity.

00:07:40.335 --> 00:07:44.354
PMR-SPARE tested tocilizumab, which blocks the same receptor, in

00:07:44.454 --> 00:07:49.117
new-onset PMR: sixty-three versus twelve percent in steroid-free remission

00:07:49.217 --> 00:07:52.978
at sixteen weeks. But with thirty-six participants, it can't

00:07:53.078 --> 00:07:54.909
tell us much about rare harms.

00:07:55.502 --> 00:07:59.604
And in twenty twenty-six, REPLENISH tested secukinumab, which targets a

00:07:59.704 --> 00:08:03.985
different cytokine, IL-17A. About forty-one percent on either dose, versus

00:08:04.085 --> 00:08:07.714
twenty percent on placebo, stayed in remission from week twelve

00:08:07.814 --> 00:08:10.911
to fifty-two. Infections were more common on the drug.

00:08:11.802 --> 00:08:15.579
Different people, endpoints and tapers mean these bars can't be

00:08:15.679 --> 00:08:19.271
compared across trials. For any trial, ask: who was studied,

00:08:19.371 --> 00:08:22.533
what counted as success, and what came with the drug?

00:08:24.293 --> 00:08:27.354
Many people recover, but not on a two-year script.

00:08:27.454 --> 00:08:31.338
In a pooled analysis of real-world cohorts, about seventy-seven

00:08:31.438 --> 00:08:34.942
percent were still taking glucocorticoids after one year,

00:08:35.026 --> 00:08:39.187
fifty-one percent after two years, and twenty-five percent after five.

00:08:39.259 --> 00:08:42.816
A guide to expectations, not a prediction for anyone.

00:08:43.393 --> 00:08:46.690
The same review found about forty-three percent relapsed at least

00:08:46.790 --> 00:08:49.356
once in the first year. Relapse is a common feature

00:08:49.456 --> 00:08:51.709
of the disease, and never a personal failure.

00:08:52.393 --> 00:08:56.479
There's reassurance too: a Norwegian cohort, over a thirty-eight-year

00:08:56.579 --> 00:08:59.938
study period, found no increase in overall mortality with

00:09:00.038 --> 00:09:04.064
PMR. And rebuilding strength and function, dressing, walking, rising

00:09:04.164 --> 00:09:06.491
from a chair, is part of getting better.

00:09:06.463 --> 00:09:09.791
To put it together: PMR is inflammation around the joints,

00:09:09.891 --> 00:09:13.457
not destroyed muscle. It involves immune signals such as IL-6,

00:09:13.557 --> 00:09:17.240
follows a daily rhythm, and is diagnosed by assembling evidence.

00:09:17.329 --> 00:09:21.225
Treatment controls it first, then reduces carefully, with two

00:09:21.325 --> 00:09:24.237
clocks in mind. And the symptoms of giant cell

00:09:24.337 --> 00:09:27.840
arteritis, especially any change in vision, never wait.

00:09:28.529 --> 00:09:31.373
If you or someone you care about lives with PMR, questions

00:09:31.473 --> 00:09:34.571
like these make a good starting point for the next appointment.

00:09:34.663 --> 00:09:38.191
And remember: this isn't medical advice. Never stop long-term

00:09:38.291 --> 00:09:41.760
steroids suddenly, and new vision symptoms are an emergency.

00:09:42.429 --> 00:09:45.090
The full interactive guide, with every source,

00:09:45.190 --> 00:09:47.611
lives in the Almanac. Thanks for watching.
